course: biology
lesson: 71
title: Growing: teeth, height, hair, nails, from baby to grown-up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body is a building site that never closes: bones lengthen, teeth are swapped, hair and nails creep out, and most of the building work happens while you sleep.
explain: Look at the pencil marks on the kitchen door frame: each birthday line is a few centimetres higher, because soft growth plates near the ends of your long bones keep adding new bone until your late teens.
explain: The parts you cut, hair and nails, are not alive, which is why the barber's scissors and Sunday nail clippers never hurt; the living part hides in the skin, out of reach.
explain: In cartoons a character drinks a potion and shoots up overnight, or a fright turns hair white in a second; real growing is slow, steady and full of surprises, like being taller in the morning than at bedtime.
fact: Children have 20 baby teeth; the grown-up tooth underneath pushes up while the root of the baby tooth dissolves, and adults usually end up with 32 teeth counting four wisdom teeth, which may come late or never; a lost adult tooth never grows back.
fact: Long bones grow at soft growth plates near their ends, told to work by hormones from the pea-sized pituitary gland under the brain; when the plates harden in the late teens, a person stops getting taller.
fact: The soft discs between the bones of the spine get squashed during the day and puff up again at night, so most people are about 1 centimetre taller just after waking, and astronauts in space grow a few centimetres taller for a while.
fact: Nails are made of keratin, like hair and rhino horn; the part you cut has no nerves, and fingernails grow about 3 millimetres a month while toenails grow only about 1 millimetre a month.
fact: Each hair grows from a follicle in the skin, about 1 centimetre a month; we lose about 50 to 100 hairs a day, and cutting hair does not make it grow back thicker or faster.
fact: A baby's skull has soft gaps called fontanelles that close in the first two years; a newborn has around 300 bones and soft parts, and many join so that a grown-up has 206 bones.
fact: Many children aged about 3 to 12 get aching legs at night that are gone by morning; they are called growing pains, but doctors are not sure growth causes them, as bones grow very slowly and tired muscles may be part of it, and limping, swelling or redness need a doctor.
fact: In deep sleep the body releases most of its growth hormone, and children aged 9 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night.
fact: Babies usually walk on their own somewhere between about 9 and 18 months, while a foal or a baby giraffe stands within an hour of birth; a human baby's brain still has a lot of growing to do, and it must learn balance.
fact: In the teenage years the voice box grows and the vocal folds get longer and thicker, so the voice gets deeper and may jump about for a while; the change is often bigger in boys.
fact: Feet grow fast in childhood, often a size or more a year, and usually stop growing in the early to mid teens, before the rest of the body.
fact: Hair goes grey when the colour cells in the follicles make less melanin with age; hair that has already grown cannot turn white overnight from a fright.
exercise: What pushes a wobbly baby tooth out? | The grown-up tooth growing underneath it | A fresh baby tooth growing beside it in the gum | Eating crunchy apples every single day | The tooth fairy loosening it at night
exercise: How many teeth does a grown-up usually end up with, counting wisdom teeth? | 32 | 20 | 28 | 40
exercise: Where do long bones like the thigh bone get longer? | At growth plates near their ends | In the very middle of the bone shaft | Only at the knee joint, nowhere else | All along the outside of the bone
exercise: Why does a person stop getting taller in the late teens? | The growth plates harden into solid bone | The bones simply run out of calcium to use for growing | The skin becomes too tight to stretch any more | The heart stops pumping special growth blood
exercise: Why are you a little taller in the morning than at bedtime? | The discs in your spine puff up again while you lie down | Your bones grow a little more every single night while you dream | Your feet swell up while you are asleep | Your head stretches on the pillow
exercise: Why does clipping your fingernails not hurt? | The part you cut has no nerves and is not alive | Nails are made of numb, cold bone with no blood in it | The pain goes away too quickly to feel | Fingers switch off their nerves on Sundays
exercise: Which grow faster? | Fingernails, about three times faster than toenails | Toenails, about three times faster than fingernails | Both grow at exactly the same speed | Neither grows after you are ten years old
exercise: About how fast does hair grow? | About 1 centimetre a month | About 1 centimetre every day | About 1 centimetre every year | About 10 centimetres a month
exercise: What is the soft spot on top of a baby's head? | A gap between skull bones that closes in the first two years | A thin patch of skin where the brain is left open to the air until school | A hole that stays open for the whole of life | A bruise that every baby gets at birth
exercise: How many bones does a grown-up have? | 206 | 300 | 106 | 360
exercise: What is true about growing pains? | They come at night, go by morning, and may not be caused by growing | They are the bones cracking a little as they stretch longer each night | They only happen to grown-ups after sport | They always cause limping and swelling
exercise: When does the body release most of its growth hormone? | During deep sleep | During a big breakfast | During long PE lessons | During the hottest part of the day
exercise: How much sleep does a child of 9 to 12 need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 5 to 6 hours a night | About 7 hours, like most grown-ups | About 14 to 16 hours
exercise: Roughly when do most babies take their first steps on their own? | Between about 9 and 18 months | Within an hour of being born | At about 3 years old | At about 3 months old
exercise: Why does a teenager's voice get deeper? | The voice box grows and the vocal folds get longer | The lungs shrink and push out less air, so the sound drops lower | The tongue gets thicker and slower | The teeth change and block the sound
exercise: When do feet usually stop growing? | In the early to mid teens, before the rest of the body | At the age of five, before school starts | Never, feet keep growing all your life | In the late twenties, long after the rest of the body
exercise: Why does hair turn grey? | Colour cells in the follicles make less melanin with age | Hair gets bleached by many years of sunlight on the head | Old hair fills up with tiny bubbles of dust from the air | Worry turns each hair white overnight
test: «got my hair cut short so it grows back thicker, everyone knows that» What is right? | That's a myth; scissors never touch the follicle where hair grows | Yes, each cut doubles the number of hairs on your head, so keep cutting | Yes, but only if you cut it at night | No, cutting hair stops it growing for a month
test: In a cartoon, a boy drinks a potion and grows a metre taller overnight. What does real growing look like? | A few centimetres a year, added slowly at the growth plates | A whole metre every year until you turn ten, then it stops | Growing only on birthdays, all at once | Growing only in summer, never in winter
test: «Grandad says he went white overnight when the dog bit him» What is right? | Hair already grown cannot lose its colour overnight; grey comes slowly | Yes, a really big fright bleaches every single hair on the head at once | Yes, but only the hairs on the left side | No, his hair turned black overnight instead
test: What would happen if you measured an astronaut just back from months in space? | They would be a few centimetres taller for a while, then shrink back | They would be exactly the same height as before | They would be much shorter for the rest of their life, as space squashes bones | They would keep growing taller on Earth for years
test: «my legs ache every night after football, it's my bones snapping longer innit» What is right? | No, bones grow very slowly; tired muscles may be part of it | Yes, bones grow a centimetre every night with a snap, that's the ache | Yes, and you should stop playing sport for good | No, it is your toenails growing too fast
test: «sod it, I'll stay up till 1, sleep's a waste of time» What is the best answer? | Sleep is when most growth hormone is released and the brain sorts what you learned | Sleep does nothing, so staying up is fine | You only grow while you are awake, running and moving about in the daytime, never at night | Grown-ups need more sleep than children anyway
test: Mum says your new school shoes already pinch after one year. What is the most likely reason? | Your feet grew a size or more, as children's feet often do | Shoes shrink in the wash every week | Your feet swell from too much sleep | Toenails grow faster than feet and push the shoes
test: Why can a foal stand an hour after birth while a human baby takes about a year to walk? | A human baby's brain still has a lot of growing to do and must learn balance | Human babies are just lazier than foals and would rather be carried about by their parents | Human legs have no bones until age one | Foals are born already one year old
test: «my cousin's voice went squeaky mid-sentence lol is he ill» What is right? | No, his voice box is growing and the voice jumps about for a while | Yes, a squeaky voice means a bad sore throat | Yes, his lungs are shrinking as he grows | No, he is copying a cartoon on purpose
test: «baby's head has a soft bit, crap, is it broken??» What is right? | No, it is a normal gap between skull bones that closes by about two | Yes, the baby must have fallen out of bed and cracked its head open | Yes, babies are born with half a skull | No, it is a hard lump that comes with age
test: «the tooth I lost last week'll grow back, it was a big one» What is right? | A lost adult tooth never grows back, only baby teeth are replaced | All teeth grow back as long as you brush them twice a day without fail | Teeth grow back every ten years in grown-ups | Only front teeth grow back, not the side ones
exam: Identify the principal anatomical site at which the long bones of a child increase in length. | The growth plates situated near the extremities of the bone | The central portion of the bone shaft | The outer surface of the bone along its entire length | The joint cartilage of the knee exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the observed variation in human stature over the course of a single day. | Stature is greater upon waking, owing to re-expansion of the spinal discs during rest | Stature is greater at bedtime, owing to the growth of the bones in the course of the day | Stature is invariable throughout the day and night | Stature is greater at midday, owing to the warmth of the sun
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cutting of hair and nails? | The portions removed are composed of keratin, contain no nerves and are not alive | The portions removed are living tissue richly supplied with both nerves and blood vessels | Cutting causes subsequent growth to be thicker and more rapid | Cutting causes the follicle to cease production permanently
exam: State the number of bones possessed by the adult human skeleton. | Two hundred and six | Three hundred | One hundred and six | Three hundred and sixty
